content.text:
Both requests are resolved:

**1. Inclusion confirmed.** `tsconfig.json`'s default `include` already covers `**/*.ts`/`**/*.tsx`, which matches `src/types/mantine.d.ts` — no explicit `files`/`include` entry needed. I verified this directly by building the actual TS program from the tsconfig and checking `theme.other.playerNameMinWidthSingleLine` / `...MultiLine`: both resolve to the augmented type everywhere, not `any`/`unknown`.

**2. Retyped as CSS length.** Changed both properties in `src/types/mantine.d.ts` from plain `string` to `CSSProperties['minWidth']` (imported from `react`), so they now accept any valid CSS min-width value (keywords like `'auto'`, `'fit-content'`, numbers, etc.) rather than an unconstrained string:

```ts
import type { CSSProperties } from 'react';

export { };

declare module '@mantine/core' {
    export interface MantineThemeOther {
        /** Minimum width of a player name column, so the name fits on one line. */
        playerNameMinWidthSingleLine: CSSProperties['minWidth'];
        /** Minimum width of a player name column, so the name fits on multiple lines. */
        playerNameMinWidthMultiLine: CSSProperties['minWidth'];
    }
}
```

`typecheck`, `lint`, `knip`, and the `PlayerList`/`PlayerWDLChart` unit tests all still pass.

One thing worth flagging while I was in there: `playerNameMinWidthMultiLine` is currently applied via the `w` prop in `PlayerList.tsx` (an exact width), not `miw` (min-width) — despite the token's name. Mantine types both as `StyleProp<CSSProperties['width' | 'minWidth']>` respectively, so it compiles fine either way, but the prop choice doesn't match the "min width" semantics of the name. Want me to switch it to `miw`, or is `w` intentional (e.g. paired with `layout="fixed"` to pin an exact column width rather than a floor)?

